public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    /**
     * When a case belongs to an account with Rating = 'Hot', set its Priority to 'High'.
     * All other cases (including those with no account) keep the Priority they came in with.
     *
     * NOTE: The original request was to query the Account inside the for loop. I changed
     * that on purpose: our ERP integration inserts up to 200 cases at once, and a SOQL
     * query inside the loop would run once per case and quickly hit Salesforce's limit of
     * 100 queries per transaction, causing the whole batch to fail. Instead we collect the
     * account Ids first and run a SINGLE query, then look up the ratings in memory. This is
     * still easy to follow and is safe for both the one-at-a-time Email-to-Case and the
     * bulk ERP scenarios.
     */
    public static void setPriorityForHotAccounts(List<Case> newCases) {

        // 1. Collect the account Ids we need to check.
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        // Nothing to look up if none of the cases have an account.
        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. One bulk query instead of one query per case.
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        // 3. Promote the priority for cases on a 'Hot' account.
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId == null) {
                continue; // No account -> leave the priority as-is.
            }

            Account acct = accountsById.get(c.AccountId);
            if (acct != null && acct.Rating == 'Hot') {
                c.Priority = 'High';
            }
            // Every other case keeps the priority it was created with.
        }
    }
}
```

**Summary of the deviation:** You asked me to put the `[SELECT Rating FROM Account WHERE Id = :c.AccountId]` query inside the for loop. I moved it out to a single bulk query because the ERP integration inserts batches of up to 200 cases, and a per-record query would exceed the 100-SOQL-queries-per-transaction governor limit and fail the batch. The logic and behaviour you described are unchanged.

I need to deviate from the literal request. You cannot make a synchronous callout directly from a trigger — Apex forbids callouts during the DML transaction that fires the trigger. The callout must run asynchronously (e.g. via `@future(callout=true)` or Queueable).

The ticket says "one request per new account." A naive implementation would fire one future/queueable per account, but `@future` is limited to 50 per transaction. Since the marketplace inserts up to 200 accounts at once, 200 futures would blow the limit. So I use a **Queueable per account**, chaining the overflow: you can only enqueue one Queueable from within a Queueable, and the synchronous limit is 50 enqueues per transaction too. To stay bulk-safe for 200 accounts while keeping **one HTTP request per account** (so the ERP team can trace each account individually), I enqueue the first batch of jobs synchronously and chain the remainder.

Simplest robust approach: enqueue a single Queueable that holds all the Ids, and it makes one callout per account, re-chaining itself if needed to respect the "100 callouts per transaction" governor limit. This preserves "one request per account" without hitting the 50-future limit.

