I'd do it differently: the nested loop is O(leads × contacts), which for 3,000 × 3,000 is 9 million comparisons plus a string normalization inside the inner loop — wasteful and close to CPU-time risk inside an import transaction that's already doing other work. A single pass to build a normalized-email → Contact Id map makes the whole thing O(leads + contacts) with no change in readability. So I'll build the map first, then do one lookup per lead.

Key points honored:
- Same size and order as `leads`.
- Case-insensitive, trimmed email matching.
- Blank emails (null/empty/whitespace) never match → `null`.
- First contact in `contacts` order wins (so I only put a normalized email into the map if it's not already present).
- No SOQL/DML; pure in-memory work.

