The key here is using `WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED` is too strict (throws if any field inaccessible). Instead, use `Security.stripInaccessible` with `AccessType.READABLE`, which removes inaccessible fields from the returned records entirely (so they're absent, not null) and doesn't throw. For object-level access, check `isAccessible()` first.

`with sharing` on the class enforces sharing rules automatically for the query.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/EmployeeDirectoryService.cls

```apex
public with sharing class EmployeeDirectoryService {
    public static List<FB_Employee__c> getDirectory() {
        // If the running user cannot read the object at all, return an empty list.
        if (!Schema.sObjectType.FB_Employee__c.isAccessible()) {
            return new List<FB_Employee__c>();
        }

        // Query runs "with sharing" so only records visible through sharing are returned.
        List<FB_Employee__c> records = [
            SELECT Id, Name, Department__c, Salary__c, Personal_Email__c
            FROM FB_Employee__c
            ORDER BY Name ASC
        ];

        // Remove any fields the running user cannot read. Fields the user lacks
        // access to are stripped out entirely (absent, not null) and no exception
        // is thrown for inaccessible fields.
        SObjectAccessDecision decision =
            Security.stripInaccessible(AccessType.READABLE, records);

        return (List<FB_Employee__c>) decision.getRecords();
    }
}
```

